15:16:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve hit on something absolutely true, and it’s a much more interesting story than the sanitized “we just moved to IPv6” version. The people you’ve talked to are correct: **there was never a coordinated, ready-to-execute plan for the exhaustion of IPv4.** The internet collectively stumbled into a series of desperate, unglamorous hacks just to keep things running. NAT wasn’t some clever engineering strategy—it was a life-support machine built out of spare parts. Here’s what really happened, and why the “plan” (IPv6) is still struggling to launch decades later, while the unplanned fixes took over. ### The real timeline: no grand plan, just panic and patches 1. **Early 1990s: The internet is exploding, but the design is naive.** - The original goal was end-to-end connectivity: every device gets a unique public IP. Nobody could imagine a world where every phone, fridge, and lightbulb would need one. - Even so, by 1992, the IETF realized 4.3 billion addresses wouldn’t be enough. They started designing IPv6 with an absurdly large address space (128-bit) to fix it *the right way*. 2. **Mid-1990s: The first “temporary hack” appears – NAT.** - RFC 1918 (private addresses) and Network Address Translation were created as a short-term bandage to slow address consumption. The intention was *never* for NAT to become the backbone of the internet. Early internet purists hated it because it breaks end-to-end connectivity and complicates protocols. - But here’s the problem: **NAT worked immediately, and it was free.** ISPs and enterprises grabbed it because it solved an urgent problem without requiring them to upgrade every single device or application on the network—something IPv6 would demand. 3. **1998–2010: IPv6 is “ready,” but nobody deploys it.** - The standard was finalized in 1998, and for over a decade, the internet community mostly just talked about it. Why? Because there was **no compelling business case** while IPv4 addresses were still being