IPv6** not because they wanted to, but because they saw their traffic increasingly coming from CGNAT middleboxes that broke geolocation, logging, and performance. They enabled IPv6 to improve user experience, which slowly created the critical mass we see today. In short, your contacts are dead right. The internet didn’t execute a pre-cooked plan for exhaustion. It **backed into a series of increasingly invasive, unplanned fixes** (NAT → CGNAT → address trading → protocol translation) just to survive the day. The official “plan” (IPv6) has been waiting in the wings for 25 years, and it’s only now being deployed in earnest because the hacks themselves are reaching their breaking point. The resilience of the internet isn’t born from a master blueprint; it’s from an ugly, pragmatic tradition of patching the plane while it’s in flight.
